calculate the reverse split ratio.

.why = the ratio determines how many old shares become one new share.

---

## formula

```
ratio = issued shares ÷ target shares
```

example:
- issued: 8,000,000
- target: 1,000
- ratio: 8,000,000 ÷ 1,000 = **8000:1**

---

## calculate yours

| field | value |
|-------|-------|
| current issued | |
| target issued | |
| **ratio** | :1 |

---

## par value adjustment

one common treatment scales par by the same ratio, so stated capital is
unchanged:

| field | before | after |
|-------|--------|-------|
| shares | 8,000,000 | 1,000 |
| par value | $0.00001 | $0.08 |
| total value | $80 | $80 |

> 🚩 **this section read "par value increases by the same ratio" until
> 2026-09-02** — stated as a mechanical certainty, uncited, in the stone whose
> output becomes a **filed Certificate of Amendment**.
>
> ⛔ **whether Delaware requires the par change is NOT established in this
> repo.** a reverse split that holds par constant reduces stated capital, which
> raises a § 244 question this route has never pulled. do not treat the table
> above as the required form.

✅ **what IS measured: the choice does not move the tax.** at 5,000 authorized /
1,000 issued / $50,000 gross assets, `franchise.tax.delaware` returns **$175.00**
under **both** treatments —

| par after the split | annual tax |
|---|---:|
| $0.08 (scaled) | **$175.00** |
| $0.00001 (held constant) | **$175.00** |

— because the Authorized Shares Method governs at ≤5,000 authorized and *"does
not count assets at all."* so the franchise-tax goal of this route gives you **no
reason to prefer either treatment**, and the decision belongs to whoever drafts
the amendment.

calculate yours:

| field | before | after |
|-------|--------|-------|
| shares | | |
| par value | $ | $ |
| total value | $ | $ |

